      {
        "id": "p82-q54-part-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $x^2+x+2>0$ for all values of $x$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\Delta=1^2-4(1)(2)=-7<0$. The coefficient of $x^2$ is positive, so $x^2+x+2>0$ for all real $x$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=1^2-4(1)(2)\\\\\\Delta&=-7<0\\end{align*}$$\n\nThere are no real zeroes and the coefficient of $x^2$ is positive. Hence $x^2+x+2>0$ for all real $x$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p82-q54-part-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, solve $\\dfrac{3x+5}{x^2+x+2}\\leq1$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x\\leq-1$ or $x\\geq3$.",
          "worked": "By part (a), $x^2+x+2>0$. Multiply both sides by this denominator.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}3x+5&\\leq x^2+x+2\\\\x^2-2x-3&\\geq0\\\\(x-3)(x+1)&\\geq0\\end{align*}$$\n\nThus $x\\leq-1$ or $x\\geq3$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      }
    ]
  },
